Nice — this is a classic real SRE troubleshooting scenario question.
They are not testing Kubernetes commands… they are testing your thinking order.

Pod is created but goes UP and DOWN intermittently

This usually means CrashLoopBackOff / failing health checks / resource issue / dependency issue

You must answer in a structured debug flow 👇

🧠 Golden Rule (Say this first in interview)

I will debug from Kubernetes → Container → Application → Dependency → Infrastructure

Interviewers LOVE this line.

Step-by-Step Debugging Flow
1️⃣ Check Pod Status

First understand how it is failing.

kubectl get pods -n <ns> -o wide
kubectl describe pod <pod>

Look for:

Field	Meaning
CrashLoopBackOff	App crashing
OOMKilled	Memory issue
Error	Container exit
Running but restarting	Health probe failing
2️⃣ Check Events (MOST IMPORTANT)

In describe output scroll to bottom:

Events:
Back-off restarting failed container
Readiness probe failed
Liveness probe failed
OOMKilled
ImagePullBackOff

This alone often answers the question.

3️⃣ Check Logs

Current container logs:

kubectl logs <pod>

Previous crash logs:

kubectl logs <pod> --previous

💡 Interview tip:

For intermittent pods, --previous is critical because container already restarted.

4️⃣ Health Probe Issues (Very Common)

If pod alternates UP/DOWN → usually liveness probe killing it.

Check:

kubectl describe pod <pod> | grep -A5 Liveness

Typical problems:

Wrong path /health

App slow startup

Port mismatch

DB dependency not ready

Fix:
Increase:

initialDelaySeconds
timeoutSeconds
failureThreshold
5️⃣ Resource Issues

Check if node killing container:

kubectl top pod
kubectl describe pod | grep -i oom

If OOMKilled:

memory limit too low or memory leak

6️⃣ Exec Into Pod (If running briefly)
kubectl exec -it <pod> -- sh

Check:

config files

env variables

DNS resolution

service connectivity

Example:

curl db-service
nslookup redis
7️⃣ Dependency Failures

Very common real issue:

Pod starts → tries DB → fails → exits → restart → works → fails again

Check service:

kubectl get svc
kubectl get endpoints
8️⃣ Node Issues

Sometimes pod fine but node problem:

kubectl describe node <node>

Look for:

memory pressure

disk pressure

network plugin issues

Most Common Root Causes (Mention These)

Liveness probe killing slow app

DB/Redis dependency not reachable

OOMKilled due to wrong limits

ConfigMap/Secret missing

Wrong environment variables

Port mismatch

Application crash

Perfect Interview Answer (Say this)

Since the pod is intermittently going down, I suspect CrashLoopBackOff or failing liveness probe. I will first check pod events using describe, then check previous container logs, verify health probes, inspect resource limits for OOMKilled, and finally validate dependencies like DB or services connectivity from inside the container.


