Excellent — now you’re thinking like an SRE 👌
Yes, interviewer expects this follow-up.

“You have 200 pods… you won’t debug one-by-one. So how do you quickly identify the bad ones?”

They want filtering + pattern detection.

Step 1 — Find Only Problematic Pods (Don’t Check All)
Show only failing pods
kubectl get pods -n <ns> | grep -v Running

OR better:

kubectl get pods -n <ns> --field-selector=status.phase!=Running

Now you reduced 200 → maybe 5 pods.

Find restarting pods (VERY IMPORTANT)
kubectl get pods -n <ns> --sort-by=.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount

Highest restart count = real problem pod 🔥

Step 2 — Group Debugging Instead of One-by-One

Usually pods belong to same deployment.

Get owner:

kubectl get pod <pod> -o jsonpath='{.metadata.ownerReferences[0].name}'

Then check deployment:

kubectl describe deploy <deployment>
kubectl rollout status deploy <deployment>

If multiple pods failing → issue is application/config, not pod.

Step 3 — Check Events for Whole Namespace (Power Move)

Instead of per-pod:

kubectl get events -n <ns> --sort-by=.lastTimestamp

You instantly see pattern:

Readiness probe failed
Readiness probe failed
Readiness probe failed

Boom → probe issue.

Step 4 — Check Logs for ALL Pods Together
kubectl logs -l app=myapp -n <ns> --tail=50

Now you read logs of every replica at once.

Step 5 — Metrics Based Detection (Very Senior Answer)

Instead of kubectl:

In Prometheus/Grafana check:

container restarts

OOM kills

error rate

latency spike

You don’t even SSH or kubectl first.

Perfect Interview Answer (Memorize This)

In large environments I won’t debug pod by pod. First I filter non-running or high-restart pods, then check namespace events to identify patterns, then inspect the deployment and aggregated logs. Usually if multiple replicas fail, it indicates configuration or dependency issue rather than individual pod failure.

One-Line Senior Version 😄

Debug the pattern, not the pod.




